<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Rugians</h1><p class="st-meta">410 CE – 487 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small East Germanic kingdom north of the Danube in Lower Austria, known chiefly from the Life of Saint Severinus and destroyed by Odoacer in 487.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Rugii originally lived on the Baltic coast, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> places them. After migrating south and serving under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, they settled after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila's</a> death in 453 on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> opposite the Roman province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-noricum">Noricum</a>, in the area later called Rugiland, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Rugian kings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arian Christians</a> who dominated the remaining Roman towns of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-noricum">Noricum</a> Ripense, collecting tribute and controlling trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugippius">Eugippius</a>' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-saint-severinus">Life of Saint Severinus</a> portrays King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flaccitheus">Flaccitheus</a>, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feletheus">Feletheus</a> and the harsh Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giso">Giso</a>, whom the saint rebuked. Their power depended on balancing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-heruli">Heruli</a> and the Roman population.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 487 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, attacked Rugiland and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feletheus">Feletheus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giso">Giso</a>; in 488 his brother crushed their son Fredericus, and the Roman population of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-noricum">Noricum</a> was evacuated to Italy. Surviving Rugians joined Theodoric's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and went with them to Italy, keeping a separate identity there into the 540s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-rugians.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Rugians" width="320" height="291"><figcaption>Image: Skäpperöd · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rugii.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>410 CE – 487 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Flaccitheus, Feletheus, Fredericus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>East Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rugiland, Rugii</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=448&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-rugians" aria-label="Kingdom of the Rugians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5006 515 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of the Rugians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5292 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5326 771h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5326 771h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Austria. <a href="/atlas?year=448&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-rugians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

